db/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087940 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087940 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087940 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087940 #edb/ref 1045 "Parametric Equations of Parabolas" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087940 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186088695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087940 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087940 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087940 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186087940 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "388f5ace-d291-564e-ba4f-c8bd5e29bb8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088435 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088435 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84731" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088435 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088435 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088435 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "50547e51-0957-5746-a2ba-be9442cc3fec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088435 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =4x,\\,t=2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=4x$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2} & =4x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088687 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088687 #edb/ref 1061 27576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088687 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "86afef72-d424-5e00-9cca-2deb22614b51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088687 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088688 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088688 #edb/ref 1061 27577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088688 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a81f2327-ee3f-53ff-8ff9-4e5727353be6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088688 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088689 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088689 #edb/ref 1061 27586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088689 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d0e480ce-44cf-566d-a958-fb2ee799eb46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088689 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088695 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088695 #edb/ref 1061 28491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088695 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "283e44b0-129c-5ed3-8eda-f67b74ce0e0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088695 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088696 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088696 #edb/ref 1061 28492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088696 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3c903871-8fc1-5bba-b0a9-757980a0ad09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088696 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088697 #edb/ref 1061 28495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088697 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f517edbc-d6fc-5d91-ab9f-1f40acb5734e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088697 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088970 #edb/ref 1064 "The standard parametric equations of a *vertical* parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = 2pt, \\qquad y = pt^2, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nAs usual, the sign of $p$ determines whether the parabola is upward-opening or downward-opening.\n\n- When $p > 0,$ the parabola is upward-opening.\n\n![](images/39/3962fbc2a0fff44300677c89e867c57582473a8e56aedfe0fcf1d20fb9a322c9.png)\n\n- When $p < 0,$ the parabola is downward-opening.\n\n![](images/63/63acc881f875fbf96fb6ef8a8db9fb778669eabbc171e3800e521a48c130aa89.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088970 #edb/ref 1065 4624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088970 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a0b858e3-ea45-5f93-903c-65d346ab9b0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088970 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088971 #edb/ref 1064 "The parametric equations of a *horizontal* parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = pt^2, \\qquad y = 2pt, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nThis time, the sign of $p$ determines whether the parabola is left-opening or right-opening.\n\n- When $p > 0,$ the parabola is right-opening.\n\n![](images/77/7758fc4d8eaec780a92c548393b48eb1457d6961fb33aa7e4e4e776e71a4fd7a.png)\n\n- When $p < 0,$ the parabola is left-opening.\n\n![](images/28/28f17924216890db2b0f9a6baf914502184ce21d7f5723a933c955e789b1bb23.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088971 #edb/ref 1065 4625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088971 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6480b59c-8f91-5c94-8eb9-0f1bfe519e81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088971 #edb/ref 1067 "The Parametric Equations of Horizontal Parabolas Whose Vertex Is at the Origin" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088972 #edb/ref 1064 "We've been told that the parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = 2pt, \\qquad y = pt^2, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nBut how do we know that these parametric equations describe a parabola?\n\nFirst, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2pt & ⟹ &  & t & =\\frac{x}{2p} \\\\ y & =p{t}^{2} & ⟹ &  & {t}^{2} & =\\frac{y}{p}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first equation above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4{p}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the above into the second equation $t^2 = \\dfrac{y}{p},$ we can eliminate the parameter $t$ and rearrange to find the Cartesian equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{y}{p} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4{p}^{2}} & =\\frac{y}{p} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{y}{p}\\cdot 4{p}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4py\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the Cartesian equation of a vertical parabola, which confirms that the parametric equations describe a vertical parabola.\n\nIf we followed the same procedure for the parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0),$ we would obtain that the Cartesian equation of the parabola is $y^2 = 4px.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088972 #edb/ref 1065 4626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088972 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "eeb3b2df-57fd-5962-bc60-e1e64961038e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088972 #edb/ref 1067 "How Do We Know That the Parametric Equations Describe a Parabola?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239070 #edb/ref 1080 "x=t,\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239070 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239070 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "75998071-befb-5489-be0c-bbf3e1494aea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239247 #edb/ref 1080 "x=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-{t}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239247 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239247 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "88eea04a-d8a1-51c3-97d8-6e529bd9b97e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239488 #edb/ref 1080 "x=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239488 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239488 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a4ecb594-d2f1-55cd-8617-ef560cc14075" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239743 #edb/ref 1080 "x=-t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239743 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239743 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c123727a-d48d-5583-8a9c-797904a46c03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239814 #edb/ref 1080 "x=-2t,\\,\\,y=-{t}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239814 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239814 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c7df434f-7524-5eb1-9b00-8cdfa267e631" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240425 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240425 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240425 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240425 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240425 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240425 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240425 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240425 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186239743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240425 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "336c67f8-b80c-57f5-b2fd-845c6ff24aed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241174 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241174 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106412" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241174 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241174 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c4/c42f5aac98d31b7a66a60eebae34b5e83cb62f3ba4801595a81dd9bb57706384.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241174 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f65595d4-58fb-5ba2-ab22-c9fc4bc25274" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241174 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,-8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,-8),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=2pt \\\\ -8 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{1}{2})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-t,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186292972 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{4}{5}{t}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292972 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292972 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09b142e8-d51f-5a1b-ace6-11e17bdf26a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293105 #edb/ref 1080 "-4t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293105 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293105 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "27ca048e-9b50-5dcf-9df4-14fad3d92fd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293171 #edb/ref 1080 "-2{t}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293171 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293171 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "391fb9e5-7f0b-525a-9579-6bcb09eaadd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293805 #edb/ref 1080 "8{t}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293805 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293805 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dfc786c5-1bc9-5a98-bf8d-bbc825fe9cf3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293812 #edb/ref 1080 "16t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293812 #edb/r