\frac{x}{3},\\,t=\\frac{y}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =12x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=12x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377043 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377043 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377043 #edb/ref 1069 "q-280198" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377043 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377043 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/23/23fa9b0f268e953cf9a2b770c3a3cb83c64fa0217a55e3a5313c81bf6933fc0a.png)\n\nDetermine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the above parabola. Express your answer in the form $x=2pt,y=p{t}^{2},$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377043 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "659b155d-7a86-5ce4-a60f-a888671f046c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377043 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(1,2).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,2),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=2$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2pt \\\\ 2 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =2pt \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{4p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(\\frac{1}{8})t,\\,y=(\\frac{1}{8}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186377044 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377044 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377044 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377044 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186377045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377044 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8ad9a621-dd17-56d4-9033-7136556fc8ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377045 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{4}t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377045 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377045 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b8e2d09a-689d-5e58-a00e-c11460b13e09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377046 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377046 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377046 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377046 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186377047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377046 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e0622f5b-ad86-55ca-876c-6179afbb1ce5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377047 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377047 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377047 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "843a74a1-39ae-5459-aac3-eba8e54c75d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377900 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377900 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84672" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377900 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377900 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5d/5d67b2209b622bd26ce7e4ee488ca02208bb120fcd0ab01c602db67da1291dc9.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377900 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8bf28229-05a6-5b2e-8652-9947e15e4e53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377900 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(6,3).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(6,3),$ we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=3$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6 &=2pt \\\\ 3 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =2pt \\\\ 3 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =p{(\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 3 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(3)t,\\,y=(3){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=6t,\\,y=3{t}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186377901 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377901 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377901 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377901 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377901 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377901 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377901 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377901 #edb/